.TH XUR.STD.STUB.CORECLR 7 "2026-11-23" "xur 0.1.3" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.stub.coreclr \- interact with a live CoreCLR (.NET) game
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B use std::stub::coreclr;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::stub::coreclr
provides runtime access to a CoreCLR (.NET) game's methods and objects
from inside the injected process. It is the primary module for writing
mods and trainers for Godot/.NET and other CoreCLR-based games.
.PP
Methods are resolved by assembly, type, and name, then invoked as raw
native calls. Objects are read and written at known field offsets.
Methods can be hooked with closures or captured-this references.
.PP
The runtime is not created directly; it is attached by an
engine-specific module such as
.BR std::stub::godot .
Once attached, all types in this module operate on the live process.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B null() -> ManagedObject
Return a null managed object reference.
.TP
.B from_address(addr: int) -> ManagedObject
Wrap a raw memory address as a ManagedObject. No validation is
performed; the address is trusted.
.SH Runtime METHODS
The Runtime handle is obtained from the engine module (e.g.
.BR godot::init() ).
.TP
.B method(assembly, type, name) -> Result<MethodPtr>
Resolve a managed method by assembly name, fully qualified type name,
and method name. Searches loaded assemblies first, then falls back to
metadata resolution. Fails if the method is not found.
.TP
.B find_method(assembly, type, name, param_count) -> Result<MethodPtr>
Like
.B method
but disambiguates overloads by parameter count.
Use this when a type has multiple methods with the same name.
.TP
.B load_assembly(path) -> Result<()>
Load a .NET assembly from a file path into the runtime.
.TP
.B type_names(assembly) -> Result<[str]>
List all type names in a loaded assembly.
.TP
.B method_names(assembly, type) -> Result<[str]>
List all method names on a type in a loaded assembly.
.SH MethodPtr METHODS
A MethodPtr is a resolved native function pointer to a managed method.
All call variants marshal arguments as integers (pointers) using the
System V AMD64 ABI and are only supported on x86_64.
.TP
.B call(args...) -> int
Call the method and return the raw integer result.
Arguments are marshalled as 64-bit integers.
ManagedObject arguments are passed as their address.
.TP
.B call_ptr(args...) -> ManagedObject
Call the method and wrap the result as a ManagedObject.
Use this when the method returns a managed reference.
.TP
.B call_float(args...) -> float
Call the method and return a floating-point result from the
XMM0 register.
.TP
.B call_string(args...) -> str
Call the method and read the return value as a System.String.
Equivalent to calling
.B call_ptr
followed by
.BR read_string() .
.TP
.B address() -> int
The raw native code address of the method.
.TP
.B resolve_jit() -> MethodPtr
Follow the JIT precode stub to the actual compiled method body.
Returns a new MethodPtr pointing to the resolved code.
Fails if the precode cannot be resolved (e.g. the method has
not been JIT-compiled yet).
.TP
.B patch_precode_target(new_target: MethodPtr) -> ()
Redirect the JIT precode stub to point at a different method.
This is a low-level operation for method replacement; prefer
.B hook
for most use cases.
.TP
.B capture_this() -> CapturedRef
Install a lightweight hook that saves the first argument (the
.I this
pointer) of every call into an atomic slot. The hook passes
through to the original method. Use this to capture object
references from methods you cannot call yourself, such as
singleton accessors invoked by the game's own code.
.TP
.B hook(callback) -> ()
Install a hook on the method. The callback receives the first
argument (the
.I this
pointer) as an integer and returns
.B Option<int>
\(em
.B None
passes through to the original,
.B Some(value)
overrides the return value.
.TP
.B peek_bytes(count) -> str
Read
.I count
bytes at the method address, returning a hex dump string.
Useful for inspecting JIT precode stubs.
.TP
.B read_bytes(count) -> [int]
Read
.I count
raw bytes at the method address as a byte array.
.SH ManagedObject METHODS
A ManagedObject is a reference to a live .NET object in the game's
managed heap. Field access uses explicit offsets derived from static
analysis of the game's assemblies.
.PP
.B CoreCLR object layout:
at offset +0 is the MethodTable pointer, followed by instance fields
in declaration order. Reference fields are 8-byte pointers; value
fields are packed at their natural size. Use
.BR std::re::dotnet " to discover field order from the game's DLLs."
.PP
.B System.String layout:
+0 MethodTable, +8 length (int32), +12 UTF-16 characters.
.PP
.B List<T> layout:
+0 MethodTable, +8 _items (T[] array reference), +16 _size (int32).
The backing array has: +0 MethodTable, +8 length (nint), +16 elements.
.TP
.B address() -> int
The raw memory address.
.TP
.B is_null() -> bool
Whether this is a null reference.
.TP
.B read_i64(offset) -> Result<int>
Read a 64-bit integer at the given byte offset.
.TP
.B read_i32(offset) -> Result<int>
Read a 32-bit integer at the given byte offset (sign-extended).
.TP
.B write_i32(offset, value) -> Result<()>
Write a 32-bit integer at the given byte offset.
.TP
.B read_u8(offset) -> Result<int>
Read a single unsigned byte at the given byte offset.
.TP
.B deref(offset) -> ManagedObject
Read a pointer at the given byte offset and return it as a
ManagedObject. This is the primary way to follow reference fields.
.TP
.B read_string() -> Result<str>
Read this object as a System.String. The object must point to a
valid .NET string (+8 = length, +12 = UTF-16 chars).
.TP
.B read_bytes(offset, count) -> Result<[int]>
Read
.I count
raw bytes starting at the given offset.
.SH CapturedRef METHODS
A CapturedRef is an atomic slot that holds the most recently
captured
.I this
pointer from a
.BR capture_this() " hook."
.TP
.B get() -> ManagedObject
Read the current captured reference. Returns a null ManagedObject
if no call has been intercepted yet.
.SH ARGUMENT MARSHALLING
.B call
and
.B call_ptr
marshal xur values to 64-bit integers for the System V AMD64 calling
convention:
.TP
.B int
Passed directly.
.TP
.B bool
0 or 1.
.TP
.B float
Cast to integer (truncated).
.TP
.B ManagedObject
Passed as its address.
.TP
.B ()
Passed as 0 (used for null arguments).
.PP
Return values are always raw 64-bit integers;
.B call_ptr
wraps the result as a ManagedObject, and
.B call_float
reads the XMM0 register.
.PP
Static methods take no implicit
.I this
argument. Instance methods take the object as the first argument:
.PP
.nf
// Static: ModelDb::get_AllRelics()
let relics = get_all_relics.call_ptr();

// Instance: player.get_Relics()
let list = get_relics.call_ptr(player);
.fi
.SH MEMORY SAFETY
All pointer reads are validated before access using a
.B /dev/null
write probe. Stale or invalid references return an error rather
than crashing the process. However, the GC can relocate objects
between reads, so long-lived ManagedObject values should be
refreshed each frame.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic method resolution and invocation
.nf
use std::stub::coreclr;
use std::stub::godot;

fn stub_main() {
    let rt = godot::init(9).unwrap();

    // Resolve methods
    let get_instance = rt.method("sts2", "Some.Manager", "get_Instance").unwrap();
    let get_name = rt.method("sts2", "Some.Entity", "get_Name").unwrap();

    // Static call (no this arg)
    let mgr = get_instance.call_ptr();

    // Instance call
    let name = get_name.call_string(mgr);
}
.fi
.SS Reading List<T> objects
.nf
// List<T> layout: +8 = _items (T[]), +16 = _size (int32)
// Array layout:   +8 = length (nint), +16 = elements (8 bytes each)
fn read_list(list: coreclr::ManagedObject) -> [coreclr::ManagedObject] {
    let result = [];
    if list.is_null() { return result; }
    let count = match list.read_i32(16) { Ok(c) => c, Err(_) => 0 };
    if count <= 0 { return result; }
    let items = list.deref(8);
    if items.is_null() { return result; }
    let i = 0;
    while i < count {
        let obj = items.deref(16 + i * 8);
        if !obj.is_null() { result.push(obj); }
        i += 1;
    }
    result
}
.fi
.SS Hooking with capture_this
.nf
// Capture a singleton reference from the game's own calls
let get_state = rt.method("asm", "GameState", "get_Instance").unwrap();
let captured = get_state.capture_this();

// Later, read the captured reference
let state = captured.get();
if !state.is_null() {
    let hp = state.read_i32(24).unwrap();
}
.fi
.SS Hooking with a callback
.nf
let damage = rt.method("asm", "Player", "TakeDamage").unwrap();
damage.hook(|this_addr| {
    // Skip damage entirely
    Some(0)
});
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
